WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:06.600
Before a modern jet engine ever leaves the factory floor or takes to the sky,

2
00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.600
it goes through thousands of hours of testing.

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.600
But that testing doesn't happen on a runway.

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.600
It happens inside a computer.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.600
Engineers build what they call a digital twin,

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.600
a highly detailed living computational model of that exact engine.

7
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.600
As real-world data flows in, the digital model updates,

8
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.600
letting builders see exactly how the machine will perform under intense stress.

9
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.600
Now, think about the human body.

10
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.600
Our biological systems are vastly more complicated than any machine we have ever engineered.

11
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.600
Yet, when it comes to treating illness, we rely on a surprisingly outdated approach.

12
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.600
We give patients standard, average doses based on general guidelines,

13
00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:50.600
and then we simply wait to see how they react.

14
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.600
Look at this visual comparison.

15
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.600
On the left, we map out every stress point of an engine before we push it to the limit.

16
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.600
On the right, we treat human patients using educated guesswork, hoping for the best.

17
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:06.600
A company called Instructional Biology is working to close that gap.

18
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:10.600
They are building the equivalent of a flight simulator, but for human medicine.

19
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.600
To get to a place where medical treatments are truly safe and customized for you,

20
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.600
we have to stop testing on the physical body first.

21
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.600
We need a way to test treatments on a digital model of the patient beforehand.

22
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.600
Creating that kind of simulator is a massive challenge.

23
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.600
Before you can predict how one specific person will react,

24
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.600
you first have to map out the entire biological system they belong to.

25
00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.600
This is where the Advanced Science AI Platform, or ASAP, comes in.

26
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.600
Think of ASAP as a master blueprint of human biological signaling,

27
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:46.600
charting out how our cells communicate and function.

28
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:51.600
This dashboard displays the scale of information powering that blueprint.

29
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.600
Metrics on the right show findings synthesized from 6,000 published papers,

30
00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.600
generating 50,000 mapped predictions.

31
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:03.600
On the left, a colorful circular map organizes this data into an interconnected web,

32
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:07.600
showing how disease and healing pathways interact.

33
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.600
As comprehensive as this master blueprint is, it still has one limitation.

34
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.600
It represents an average human baseline, pulling from broad population data.

35
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:20.600
Since no two patients share the exact same biology,

36
00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:25.600
a static average blueprint is not enough to confidently cure a specific individual.

37
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.600
That broad map is just the starting point.

38
00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:33.600
The next step is translating that general biology into a highly personalized model.

39
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.600
To do that, the system warps the baseline blueprint.

40
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:41.600
It pulls in your specific genetic profile, your metabolism and your medical history,

41
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.600
reshaping the standard model to match you perfectly.

42
00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.600
A true digital twin is an active living model.

43
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:52.600
Instead of a static snapshot from your last doctor's visit,

44
00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.600
it constantly updates to reflect changes in your body right now.

45
00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.600
This diagram shows how that connection is made.

46
00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:03.600
In the bottom right section, you see a wearable biosensor on the patient's arm.

47
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:07.600
That sensor acts as a bridge, feeding real-time data from the physical patient

48
00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.600
straight back into their digital model.

49
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.600
With that live connection established, a doctor can now run a precise simulation.

50
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.600
They can test a specific dose on your digital twin and watch exactly how your unique biology will react.

51
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.600
And the most important part, all of this simulation happens before a single drop of medicine

52
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.600
ever enters your actual bloodstream.

53
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:35.600
By keeping the data looping between the patient and the twin, we can spot adverse reactions early.

54
00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.600
We shift the entire trial and error process out of the hospital bed and into the digital realm.

55
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:45.600
Placing so much trust in a simulation requires strict safety boundaries.

56
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:51.600
The AI system maps the pathways and proposes treatments, but human physicians always review the evidence,

57
00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.600
set the hard limits and make the final decisions.

58
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:58.600
There is also a physical fail-safe built directly into the delivery process.

59
00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:04.600
If the wearable sensor detects your body reacting in a way that falls outside those doctor-approved limits,

60
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:08.600
the device immediately halts the flow of medicine.

61
00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:13.600
Now imagine taking this level of individual precision and scaling it up.

62
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.600
What happens when you have thousands of these digital twins running at the same time?

63
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.600
You get virtual cohorts.

64
00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:26.600
Instead of recruiting thousands of people for a physical clinical trial and waiting years for the results,

65
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:34.600
researchers can test a new drug across a massive grid of unique digital twins to predict its success almost instantly.

66
00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:37.600
This workflow illustrates the complete loop.

67
00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:45.600
The insights from those digital simulations guide how biological medicines are manufactured, made safe and finally delivered.

68
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:53.600
As shown by the dotted line at the bottom, the data from that delivery flows directly back to the beginning to inform the next round of discovery.

69
00:04:53.600 --> 00:05:03.600
By mapping biological signals and pairing them with real-time digital testing, Instructional Biology replaces standard average doses with precise calculation.

70
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.600
We finally arrive at a point where medical treatment is built, tested and delivered exclusively for you.
